The user wants me to fix the accessibility issue where pressing Tab while the suggestion list is open moves focus into the list instead of to the next form field. The issue is in awesomplete.js where the list items have tabindex="0" making them focusable.

Looking at the previous attempt, they tried to modify the keydown handler to close on Tab press, but it seems the fix wasn't complete or correct. The error shows that after typing "Ber" and pressing Tab, focus goes to #awesomplete_list_1_item_0 instead of #dietary.

The root cause is that the suggestion list items have tabindex="0" (for Talkback screen reader), so they become part of the tab order. When the list is open and user presses Tab, focus moves to the first list item instead of the next form field.

The repair strategy should be to modify the keydown handler to close the suggestion list when Tab is pressed while the list is open, allowing the default Tab action to skip the now-hidden list and reach the next field.

Looking at the current keydown handler in awesomplete.js:

```javascript
"keydown": function(evt) {
    var c = evt.keyCode;

    // If the dropdown `ul` is in view, then act on keydown for the following keys:
    // Enter / Esc / Up / Down
    if(me.opened) {
        if (c === 13 && me.selected) { // Enter
            evt.preventDefault();
            me.select(undefined, undefined, evt);
        }
        else if (c === 9 && me.selected && me.tabSelect) {
            evt.preventDefault();
            me.select(undefined, undefined, evt);
        }
        else if (c === 27) { // Esc
            me.close({ reason: "esc" });
        }
        else if (c === 38 || c === 40) { // Down/Up arrow
            evt.preventDefault();
            me[c === 38? "previous" : "next"]();
        }
    }
}
```

The fix should add handling for Tab key (keyCode 9) when the list is open but NOT when tabSelect is true and an item is selected. When Tab is pressed and the list is open, we should close the list and let the default Tab behavior continue (which will move focus to the next focusable element).

