}
```

But this should be before the tabSelect check, or instead of it. Actually, looking at the logic:
- If Tab is pressed and tabSelect is true and an item is selected, it selects the item (current behavior)
- Otherwise, if Tab is pressed and list is open, it should close the list and let default Tab behavior happen

So the fix is to add an else if for Tab key that closes the list when tabSelect is false or no item is selected.

Let me write the corrected keydown handler:

```javascript
"keydown": function(evt) {
    var c = evt.keyCode;

    // If the dropdown `ul` is in view, then act on keydown for the following keys:
    // Enter / Esc / Up / Down
    if(me.opened) {
        if (c === 13 && me.selected) { // Enter
            evt.preventDefault();
            me.select(undefined, undefined, evt);
        }
        else if (c === 9 && me.selected && me.tabSelect) {
            evt.preventDefault();
            me.select(undefined, undefined, evt);
        }
        else if (c === 9) { // Tab - close list and allow default tab navigation
            me.close({ reason: "tab" });
        }
        else if (c === 27) { // Esc
            me.close({ reason: "esc" });
        }
        else if (c === 38 || c === 40) { // Down/Up arrow
            evt.preventDefault();
            me[c === 38? "previous" : "next"]();
        }
    }
}
```

This way:
- If Tab is pressed with an item selected and tabSelect=true, it selects the item (existing behavior)
- If Tab is pressed otherwise (no selection or tabSelect=false), it closes the list and allows default Tab behavior to move focus to next field
- Other keys work as before

